On Sat, Sep 4, 2010 at 8:44 AM, la nub <lagelace@gmail.com> wrote:

> And just in case you were wondering, I am a programmer.
>
> Two more questions though, what do kei and ku mean?
> [...]
> these two words seem to have no meaning at all.
>

They are called "terminators" and can be some times (rather often) elided.
You can think of kei and ku as closing brackets in C or as the VB "End
While", "End If", "Next", ...
Since lojban grammar is formal as those of programming languages, you need
explicit keywords to signal the termination of a statement and avoid
ambiguities, but since the Lojban is a human language, to make it less
clumsy, you can drop a terminator whenever their presence can be inferred.
For example if a nested part ends where the main part ends, only the nested
terminater can be surely dropped.  This is a feature that the old SGML had
(if you are familiar with it).

Consider the grammar:

A -> C D
C -> letter | letter C |  "@" | ""
D -> digit D | letter D | "#" | ""

You may parse "xyt" in many different ways (i.e. "xyt" is an ambigous
statement) while "x@yt" and "xyt@" are not. So "@" is the terminator of C
(as "kei" is a terminator for "nu"). If you get "xy4" there is no ambiguity
so you don't need to waste an extra character to write "xy@4".

mu'e mi'e remod.

